the division is an online loot driven  RPG cleverly disguised as the  third-person shooter set in the grim  aftermath of a biological attack on New  York City admirably the game commits to  its grounded vision of a  medically-induced apocalypse and while  its story sticks primarily to tried and  true survival tropes the narrative is  solidly executed key moments received  full cutscenes and in-between constant  radio banter means you always know  exactly what you're doing and why that  alone goes a long way towards making  your actions feel meaningful plus it's  desolate haunting recreation in midtown  Manhattan might be the most impressive  urban world map outside of a rockstar  game with dense urban environments that  seem painstakingly assembled rather than  cut and paste it into place the variety  and authenticity of the world invite  exploration just like the streets of San  Andreas sadly the predictable AI isn't  quite as impressive no matter who you're  up against you know exactly how each  enemy is going to come at you which can  make certain lower stakes gunfights feel  like a chore some of the basic gameplay  mechanics are slightly sloppy as well  the core aiming and shooting are totally  serviceable but you can't Crouch jump or  go prone and I occasionally had to  contend with objects like poorly placed  railings obstructing my view when I was  a new cover though some of the divisions  we can't extempore others work extremely  well being able to equip any unlocked  perk on the fly for example turns the  game's extensive skill trees into  flexible strategic options the active  abilities in particular which include  seeker Minds deployable cover and a  portable health station had a  distinctive signature to the moments  moment shooting as a result the  divisions gameplay is generally fun and  engaging in spite of the enemy AI sure  they're predictable but when they're  rushing you and sniping you and trying  to blow you up all at once you still  have to dodge aim heal and get back into  cover regardless  the action is always frantic and  challenging and while that can make the  experience exhausting it can also and  frequently does trigger real adrenaline  unfortunately the adrenaline rush  occasionally slows to a drip at least  for a time most side missions are  recycled throughout the game and  eventually feel repetitive especially  since you're forced to grind through  them in an attempt to level up your  character thankfully the larger campaign  missions deliver an impressive variety  of thrilling scenarios and carefully  crafted environments I experienced a  healthy number of memorable gameplay  moments and intense boss battles and  impressively you can walk right into  these missions from the hub world with  zero loading the division also offers  four player co-op throughout but  importantly it remains fun even when  you're flying solo if you can pull  together a full four-man team though the  gameplay blossoms in unexpected ways  when you're able to revive downed  teammates cover multiple sight lines set  up planks and utilise a wider set of  character perks you realize both the  level design and the enemy AI almost  function better during the massive  firefights in - not only does the  gameplay become far more strategic you  finally feel truly unstoppable if you're  feeling a little less cooperative you  can also visit the dark zone a massive  unstructured PvP area in the middle of  the city where other players might help  you conquer the AI or they might  backstab you and steal all the stuff  you've collected the tension of not  knowing how an interaction will play out  is truly unlike anything you'll find in  a modern shooter and the significance of  loot back in the main game makes the  stakes in the dark zone feel incredibly  real to be clear loot is an integral  part of the experience your chances of  survival are heavily dictated by the  gear you equip so be prepared to spend  plenty of time in menus comparing the  numerical attributes of kneepads and  pistol holsters that may sound dry to  some but honestly Luke compelled me to  keep playing more than any other aspect  the division is relentless making you  earn every kill so when you stumble upon  a new weapon with drastically increased  stats you feel like sudden surge of  elation because you know you might  finally have an edge over your damage  soaking enemies I only wish those  moments had cropped up more often put  simply the division is stingy when it  comes to gear and XP it still manages to  feel plenty of rewarding as you progress  but a slightly kinder XP system and an  extra layer of gear rarity may have gone  a long way to alleviate the frustration  and fatigue  experience still no matter how  frustrated I grew with the game's  tiresome enemies or its demanding  leveling structure I absolutely could  not stop playing the world was too  engrossing the loot was too enticing and  the campaign was too gripping for me to  simply walk away in fact I'm gonna go  start grinding for the high-end endgame  gear right now I bind up a problem  yes good work
 

